      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Standing-seam metal roofing is the best long-term choice for Utah weather, while Class 4 architectural asphalt shingles offer the best overall value. Frame Restoration Utah",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Utah\u2019s climate features heavy winter snow loads, high-altitude UV radiation, extreme day-to-night temperature swings, freeze-thaw cycles, and strong canyon or valley winds."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Top Roofing Materials for Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Standing-Seam Metal Roofing (Best for Longevity & Snow Country)",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Pros: Naturally sheds heavy snow and ice, resists high-speed winds (110\u2013160 mph) , reflects summer heat, and lasts 50 to 70+ years with minimal maintenance."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Cons: Higher upfront installation cost (roughly 2 to 3 times the cost of asphalt)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Best for: Mountain and high-elevation areas (like Park City, Heber City, and Midway) or homeowners planning a \"forever\" home. Frame Restoration Utah +2",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  1,
                  2
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Class 4 Architectural Asphalt Shingles (Best Value for Most Homes)",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Pros: Affordable, versatile, features multi-layer durability, and provides strong wind resistance (often rated for 130 mph) . Many Utah insurance companies offer 10% to 30% policy discounts for Class 4 impact-resistant ratings."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Cons: Lifespan is shorter (roughly 25 to 35 years) and intense Utah sun/freeze-thaw cycles gradually dry out and granulate asphalt over time."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Best for: Standard residential homes along the Wasatch Front (Salt Lake City, Provo, Ogden, Layton). Top Grade Roofing Utah +2",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  3,
                  4
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Stone-Coated Steel (Best Metal Alternative)",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Pros: Combines a strong steel core with a granular stone exterior . It mimics the look of traditional shingles or tile while delivering superior hail, fire, and wind resistance, plus an air gap that cuts down on attic heat."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Cons: Expensive and requires specialized installation."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Best for: Homeowners wanting metal durability without a sleek modern panel aesthetic. High Country Metal Works +2",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  5,
                  6,
                  7
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Concrete or Clay Tile (Best for Desert & Southwestern Styles)",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Pros: Extremely long-lasting and great at handling UV rays . Concrete handles Utah freeze-thaw cycles well."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Cons: Very heavy\u2014requires a reinforced roof framing structure. Clay is more prone to cracking in northern Utah's hard freezes (better suited for southern Utah like St. George)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Best for: Southern Utah or homes engineered to carry the heavy load. shelley-construction.com +2",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  8,
                  9,
                  10
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              8,
              9,
              10
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Tips for Utah Roofs"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Check the Wind Rating: Insist on architectural or metal shingles rated for at least 130 mph to survive seasonal canyon gusts and microbursts. Top Grade Roofing Utah +1",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              11
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Prioritize Ice and Water Shields: In snowy regions, proper underlayment extended past the eaves prevents damaging ice dams during freeze-thaw cycles."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Verify Insurance Discounts: Ask your insurance carrier if upgrading to a Class 4 impact-resistant shingle qualifies you for a regional premium discount. M.W. Roofing | Utah",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
